IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hcsc-dates`

-- Health and Community Services Complaints Act 2004 (SA), with the Health and Community Services Complaints
-- Regulations 2019 where they fill in the Act (reg 4: the housing exclusion; reg 8: the prescribed offences).
-- THE RULES, seat-neutral. The questions of the person complaining are in hcsc-seat.l4, which restates none of these.
--
-- Answers are named outcomes, dates or plain text, never MAYBE BOOLEAN. Where the text says no in its own words (a
-- "means" definition, "may only be made", "does not apply", "is not binding unless", "does not constitute") the
-- outcome begins "no"; where a power is given only in stated circumstances and they are not met, the outcome says the
-- section "does not reach this"; judgments the Act leaves to the Commissioner are inputs, and the outcome is stated on
-- the condition ("if the Commissioner ...").
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on content from the South Australian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (the Act's current
-- version, 4.12.2025, and the Regulations' current version, 18.3.2019, as the register's unauthorised RTFs). State of
-- South Australia, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health and Community Services Complaints Act 2004 -- the rules`

§§ `s 4 and reg 4 -- health service and community service`

-- s 4: "health service means -- (a) ... (j) ..., but does not include -- (k) the process of writing, or the content
-- of, a health status report; (l) a service of a class excluded ... by the regulations"; "community service means --
-- (a) ... (f) ..., but does not include -- (g) a service that provides employment search or placement services, or
-- that provides employment related training or retraining; (h) a service of a class excluded ... by the regulations".
-- Both are "means" definitions. No regulation includes a further class (s 4 health service (h), community service
-- (e)); reg 4 excludes the services of a prescribed housing entity where the user can have its decision reviewed by
-- the South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal.
DECLARE `Kind of service` IS ONE OF
    `a service designed to benefit or promote human health`
    `a service with premises for the care, treatment or accommodation of aged people or people with a physical disability or mental dysfunction`
    `a diagnostic or screening service`
    `an ambulance service`
    `a service to treat or prevent illness, injury, disease or disability`
    `a service provided by a health professional`
    `a service giving information on the promotion or provision of health care or health education`
    `a social, welfare, recreational or leisure service provided as part of a health service`
    `an administration service directly related to a health service`
    `the writing or content of a health status report`
    `a service for the relief of poverty, social disadvantage, social distress or hardship`
    `a service providing emergency relief or support`
    `a service for the care or protection of a child at risk`
    `a service for the social advancement of disadvantaged groups`
    `an administration service directly related to a community service`
    `an employment search or placement service, or employment related training or retraining`
    `a housing service of the South Australian Housing Trust or a registered community housing provider`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Service answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` HAS `the service paragraph` IS A STRING
    `yes: it is a community service, s 4 definition of community service, paragraph` HAS `the service paragraph` IS A STRING
    `no: the definition of health service does not include the process of writing, or the content of, a health status report (a report on a person's health prepared for a purpose other than providing a health service) (s 4, health service (k))`
    `no: the definition of community service does not include employment search or placement services, or employment related training or retraining (s 4, community service (g))`
    `no: a housing service of the South Australian Housing Trust or a registered community housing provider is excluded where you can have its decision reviewed by the South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal (reg 4)`
    `no: it is not within the definition of health service or of community service (s 4; both are "means" definitions)`

-- Fork F2: a housing service of a prescribed housing entity that reg 4 does not exclude is taken to be a community
-- service under paragraph (a) (relief of social disadvantage or hardship); the text does not place it.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of service`, `the Tribunal can review the housing decision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Service answer`
`the s 4 service answer for` `the kind` `with Tribunal review` `the Tribunal can review the housing decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `a service designed to benefit or promote human health` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(a)"
    WHEN `a service with premises for the care, treatment or accommodation of aged people or people with a physical disability or mental dysfunction` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(b)"
    WHEN `a diagnostic or screening service` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(c)"
    WHEN `an ambulance service` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(d)"
    WHEN `a service to treat or prevent illness, injury, disease or disability` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(e)"
    WHEN `a service provided by a health professional` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(f)"
    WHEN `a service giving information on the promotion or provision of health care or health education` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(g)"
    WHEN `a social, welfare, recreational or leisure service provided as part of a health service` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(i)"
    WHEN `an administration service directly related to a health service` THEN `yes: it is a health service, s 4 definition of health service, paragraph` "(j)"
    WHEN `the writing or content of a health status report` THEN `no: the definition of health service does not include the process of writing, or the content of, a health status report (a report on a person's health prepared for a purpose other than providing a health service) (s 4, health service (k))`
    WHEN `a service for the relief of poverty, social disadvantage, social distress or hardship` THEN `yes: it is a community service, s 4 definition of community service, paragraph` "(a)"
    WHEN `a service providing emergency relief or support` THEN `yes: it is a community service, s 4 definition of community service, paragraph` "(b)"
    WHEN `a service for the care or protection of a child at risk` THEN `yes: it is a community service, s 4 definition of community service, paragraph` "(c)"
    WHEN `a service for the social advancement of disadvantaged groups` THEN `yes: it is a community service, s 4 definition of community service, paragraph` "(d)"
    WHEN `an administration service directly related to a community service` THEN `yes: it is a community service, s 4 definition of community service, paragraph` "(f)"
    WHEN `an employment search or placement service, or employment related training or retraining` THEN `no: the definition of community service does not include employment search or placement services, or employment related training or retraining (s 4, community service (g))`
    WHEN `a housing service of the South Australian Housing Trust or a registered community housing provider` THEN
        IF `the Tribunal can review the housing decision`
        THEN `no: a housing service of the South Australian Housing Trust or a registered community housing provider is excluded where you can have its decision reviewed by the South Australian Civil and Administrative Tribunal (reg 4)`
        ELSE `yes: it is a community service, s 4 definition of community service, paragraph` "(a)"
    OTHERWISE `no: it is not within the definition of health service or of community service (s 4; both are "means" definitions)`

§§ `s 24 -- who may complain`

-- s 24: "A complaint about a health or community service used, received by or sought by a person (a health or
-- community service consumer) may be made to the Commissioner by -- (a) the ... consumer; or (b) if the ... consumer
-- is a child -- (i) the child; or (ii) if the child has attained the age of 16 years -- a person appointed by the child
-- ...; or (iii) if the child has not attained the age of 16 years -- a parent or guardian of the child; or (d) the donee
-- of a power of attorney ...; (da) a substitute decision-maker ... under an advance care directive ...; (e) a person
-- who is acting on behalf of the ... consumer under another law or an order of a court; (f) a Member of Parliament;
-- (g) if the Commissioner is satisfied that it is unreasonable to expect the ... consumer to make a complaint
-- personally -- a person approved by the Commissioner ...; (h) a health or community service provider if the complaint
-- is made on the ground that the relevant service is or has been provided or is or was necessary because of the
-- actions of another ... provider; (i) if the ... consumer has died -- a person who can demonstrate to the Commissioner
-- that he or she had an enduring relationship with the deceased person, or a personal representative ...; (j) the
-- Minister; (k) the Chief Executive of the Department; (l) any other person, or any body, that, in the opinion of the
-- Commissioner, should be able to make a particular complaint in the public interest."
-- (j) and (k) are officials and have no seat. Because of (g) and (l), no one is shut out in the text's own words.
-- Fork F3: "child" is not defined in this Act; it is read as a person under 18 (Age of Majority (Reduction) Act 1970).
DECLARE `Complainant role` IS ONE OF
    `the consumer`
    `a person the consumer appointed to complain for them`
    `a parent or guardian of the consumer`
    `the donee of a power of attorney from the consumer`
    `a substitute decision-maker under the consumer's advance care directive`
    `a person acting for the consumer under another law or a court order`
    `a Member of Parliament`
    `another health or community service provider`
    `a person who had an enduring relationship with the consumer`
    `the personal representative of the consumer`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Standing answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may make the complaint under` HAS `the standing head` IS A STRING
    `yes, if you can demonstrate to the Commissioner that you had an enduring relationship with the deceased consumer (s 24(i))`
    `only if the Commissioner approves you to act for the consumer, being satisfied that it is unreasonable to expect the consumer to complain personally (s 24(g)), or is of the opinion that you should be able to make this complaint in the public interest (s 24(l))`

GIVEN `the role` IS A `Complainant role`,
      `the consumer's age` IS A NUMBER,
      `the consumer has died` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the service was needed because of another provider's actions` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner has approved me under s 24(g)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Standing answer`
`the s 24 answer for` `the role` `age` `the consumer's age` `died` `the consumer has died` `provider ground` `the service was needed because of another provider's actions` `approved` `the Commissioner has approved me under s 24(g)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the s 24 head` EQUALS ""
        THEN IF `the Commissioner has approved me under s 24(g)`
             THEN `yes: you may make the complaint under` "s 24(g)"
             ELSE `only if the Commissioner approves you to act for the consumer, being satisfied that it is unreasonable to expect the consumer to complain personally (s 24(g)), or is of the opinion that you should be able to make this complaint in the public interest (s 24(l))`
        IF `the s 24 head` EQUALS "enduring relationship"
        THEN `yes, if you can demonstrate to the Commissioner that you had an enduring relationship with the deceased consumer (s 24(i))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may make the complaint under` `the s 24 head`
    WHERE
        `the consumer is a child` MEANS `the consumer's age` LESS THAN 18
        `the s 24 head` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the role`
            WHEN `the consumer` THEN
                IF `the consumer is a child` THEN "s 24(a) and (b)(i)" ELSE "s 24(a)"
            WHEN `a person the consumer appointed to complain for them` THEN
                IF `the consumer is a child` AND (`the consumer's age` AT LEAST 16) THEN "s 24(b)(ii)" ELSE ""
            WHEN `a parent or guardian of the consumer` THEN
                IF `the consumer's age` LESS THAN 16 THEN "s 24(b)(iii)" ELSE ""
            WHEN `the donee of a power of attorney from the consumer` THEN "s 24(d)"
            WHEN `a substitute decision-maker under the consumer's advance care directive` THEN "s 24(da)"
            WHEN `a person acting for the consumer under another law or a court order` THEN "s 24(e)"
            WHEN `a Member of Parliament` THEN "s 24(f)"
            WHEN `another health or community service provider` THEN
                IF `the service was needed because of another provider's actions` THEN "s 24(h)" ELSE ""
            WHEN `a person who had an enduring relationship with the consumer` THEN
                IF `the consumer has died` THEN "enduring relationship" ELSE ""
            WHEN `the personal representative of the consumer` THEN
                IF `the consumer has died` THEN "s 24(i)" ELSE ""
            OTHERWISE ""

§§ `s 25 -- the grounds of complaint`

-- s 25(1): "A complaint may be made (and may only be made under this Act) on one or more of the following grounds: ...";
-- s 25(2): "Nothing in subsection (1) requires a health or community service provider -- (a) to act, or to provide
-- advice or information ..., outside the field of expertise associated with the provider's profession or occupational
-- group; or (b) to alter, or to hand over a copy of, medical records in the provider's possession."; s 25(4):
-- "Subsection (1)(i) does not apply in relation to the disclosure of information to -- (a) a legal practitioner in
-- connection with a related legal matter; or (b) an insurer who has an appropriate interest in the information; or (c)
-- any other person of a prescribed class." No class is prescribed. Where a ground turns on whether the provider
-- "acted unreasonably", s 85 tells the Commissioner how to judge it: the Charter, the s 22 principles, the generally
-- accepted standard, other relevant matters, and the resources reasonably available to the provider.
DECLARE `What the complaint alleges` IS ONE OF
    `the provider unreasonably did not provide, or discontinued, a service`
    `the service was not necessary or was inappropriate`
    `the provider acted unreasonably in the manner of providing the service`
    `the provider failed to exercise due skill`
    `the provider failed to treat the consumer in an appropriate professional manner`
    `the provider failed to respect the consumer's privacy or dignity`
    `the provider unreasonably failed to give the consumer enough information, choice, advice or prognosis`
    `the provider unreasonably denied or restricted access to the consumer's records`
    `the provider refused to hand over a copy of, or to alter, the consumer's medical records`
    `the provider unreasonably did not make available information about the consumer's condition`
    `the provider unreasonably disclosed information about the consumer to a third person`
    `the provider unreasonably did not deal properly with the consumer's complaint to it`
    `the provider acted inconsistently with the Charter`
    `the provider did not meet the generally accepted standard of service delivery`
    `the provider did not act or advise outside its own field of expertise`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Who received the information` IS ONE OF
    `a legal practitioner, in connection with a related legal matter`
    `an insurer with an appropriate interest in the information`
    `another person`

DECLARE `Ground answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: this is a ground of complaint under` HAS `the ground` IS A STRING
    `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` HAS `the ground` IS A STRING
    `no: s 25(1)(i) does not apply to a disclosure to a legal practitioner in connection with a related legal matter (s 25(4)(a))`
    `no: s 25(1)(i) does not apply to a disclosure to an insurer who has an appropriate interest in the information (s 25(4)(b))`
    `no: nothing in s 25(1) requires a provider to alter, or to hand over a copy of, medical records in its possession (s 25(2)(b)); denying or unreasonably restricting access to them is a ground (s 25(1)(h)(i))`
    `no: nothing in s 25(1) requires a provider to act, or to advise, outside the field of expertise of its profession or occupational group (s 25(2)(a))`
    `no: a complaint may only be made on the grounds in s 25(1)`

GIVEN `the allegation` IS A `What the complaint alleges`, `the recipient` IS A `Who received the information`
GIVETH A `Ground answer`
`the s 25 answer for` `the allegation` `disclosed to` `the recipient` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the allegation`
    WHEN `the provider unreasonably did not provide, or discontinued, a service` THEN `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(a)"
    WHEN `the service was not necessary or was inappropriate` THEN `yes: this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(b)"
    WHEN `the provider acted unreasonably in the manner of providing the service` THEN `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(c)"
    WHEN `the provider failed to exercise due skill` THEN `yes: this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(d)"
    WHEN `the provider failed to treat the consumer in an appropriate professional manner` THEN `yes: this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(e)"
    WHEN `the provider failed to respect the consumer's privacy or dignity` THEN `yes: this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(f)"
    WHEN `the provider unreasonably failed to give the consumer enough information, choice, advice or prognosis` THEN `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(g)"
    WHEN `the provider unreasonably denied or restricted access to the consumer's records` THEN `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(h)(i)"
    WHEN `the provider refused to hand over a copy of, or to alter, the consumer's medical records` THEN `no: nothing in s 25(1) requires a provider to alter, or to hand over a copy of, medical records in its possession (s 25(2)(b)); denying or unreasonably restricting access to them is a ground (s 25(1)(h)(i))`
    WHEN `the provider unreasonably did not make available information about the consumer's condition` THEN `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(h)(ii)"
    WHEN `the provider unreasonably disclosed information about the consumer to a third person` THEN
        CONSIDER `the recipient`
        WHEN `a legal practitioner, in connection with a related legal matter` THEN `no: s 25(1)(i) does not apply to a disclosure to a legal practitioner in connection with a related legal matter (s 25(4)(a))`
        WHEN `an insurer with an appropriate interest in the information` THEN `no: s 25(1)(i) does not apply to a disclosure to an insurer who has an appropriate interest in the information (s 25(4)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(i)"
    WHEN `the provider unreasonably did not deal properly with the consumer's complaint to it` THEN `yes, if the provider acted unreasonably (for the Commissioner to judge under s 85): this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(j)"
    WHEN `the provider acted inconsistently with the Charter` THEN `yes: this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(k)"
    WHEN `the provider did not meet the generally accepted standard of service delivery` THEN `yes: this is a ground of complaint under` "s 25(1)(l)"
    WHEN `the provider did not act or advise outside its own field of expertise` THEN `no: nothing in s 25(1) requires a provider to act, or to advise, outside the field of expertise of its profession or occupational group (s 25(2)(a))`
    OTHERWISE `no: a complaint may only be made on the grounds in s 25(1)`

§§ `s 25(5), (6) -- a volunteer's act`

-- s 25(5): a complaint about a volunteer's act or omission "while working for another person or body ... will be
-- taken to be a complaint against the other person or body ... and, subject to subsection (6), the volunteer cannot be
-- required to participate in any proceedings under this Act ..."; s 25(6): unless "(a) a code of conduct under section
-- 56A applies in respect of the volunteer; and (b) the Commissioner is satisfied that conduct of the volunteer poses or
-- has posed a risk to the health or safety of members of the public".
DECLARE `Volunteer answer` IS ONE OF
    `the complaint is against the provider who did it: s 25(5) is only about volunteers`
    `the complaint is taken to be against the person or body the volunteer was working for; the volunteer cannot be required to take part in proceedings under the Act (s 25(5))`
    `the complaint is taken to be against the person or body the volunteer was working for, and the volunteer may also be required to take part and be investigated, as a code of conduct applies to them and the Commissioner is satisfied their conduct poses or posed a risk to public health or safety (s 25(5), (6))`

GIVEN `a volunteer did it while working for another` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a code of conduct applies to the volunteer` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner is satisfied of a risk to the public` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Volunteer answer`
`the s 25(5) answer for` `a volunteer did it while working for another` `code` `a code of conduct applies to the volunteer` `risk` `the Commissioner is satisfied of a risk to the public` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`a volunteer did it while working for another`)
        THEN `the complaint is against the provider who did it: s 25(5) is only about volunteers`
        IF `a code of conduct applies to the volunteer` AND `the Commissioner is satisfied of a risk to the public`
        THEN `the complaint is taken to be against the person or body the volunteer was working for, and the volunteer may also be required to take part and be investigated, as a code of conduct applies to them and the Commissioner is satisfied their conduct poses or posed a risk to public health or safety (s 25(5), (6))`
        OTHERWISE `the complaint is taken to be against the person or body the volunteer was working for; the volunteer cannot be required to take part in proceedings under the Act (s 25(5))`

§§ `The regulative rules and their deadlines`

DECLARE `Complaint party` IS ONE OF
    `the complainant`
    `the Commissioner`
    `the complainant and the provider`

DECLARE `Complaint act` IS ONE OF
    `make the complaint`
    `meet the Commissioner's requirement for information, documents or a statutory declaration`
    `assess the complaint and make a determination`
    `give the complainant written notice of the determination`
    `enter into the conciliation agreement in writing`

-- s 27(1): "a complaint must be made within 2 years from the day on which the complainant first had notice of the
-- circumstances giving rise to the complaint". Clock: day 0 is that day.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complaint party` `Complaint act`
`s 27(1) -- the complaint must be made within 2 years` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY  `the complainant`
    MUST   `make the complaint`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST   BREACH BY `the complainant` BECAUSE "s 27(1): the complaint was not made within 2 years from the day the complainant first had notice (the Commissioner may extend the period under s 27(2))"

-- s 28(1), (2): the Commissioner "may, at any time, require a complainant -- (a) to provide further information or
-- documents; (b) to verify all or any part of the complaint by statutory declaration", and "must specify a reasonable
-- period within which the requirement is to be satisfied"; s 28(3): the period may be extended before or after it
-- expires. Clock: day 0 is the day of the requirement.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complaint party` `Complaint act`
`s 28 -- the requirement must be met within the period specified` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY  `the complainant`
    MUST   `meet the Commissioner's requirement for information, documents or a statutory declaration`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST   BREACH BY `the complainant` BECAUSE "s 28(2): the requirement was not met within the period specified (s 33(1)(h) may then apply)"

-- s 29(1): "The Commissioner must assess a complaint and make a determination ... within 45 days after receiving it
-- (or within such longer period as may be necessary in view of any delays that have occurred while the Commissioner or
-- another person takes steps required by or under this Act, or while the Commissioner is undertaking a preliminary
-- inquiry under section 30)." Clock: day 0 is the day of receipt.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complaint party` `Complaint act`
`s 29(1) -- the Commissioner must assess the complaint within 45 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY  `the Commissioner`
    MUST   `assess the complaint and make a determination`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST   BREACH BY `the Commissioner` BECAUSE "s 29(1): no determination within 45 days after receiving the complaint (with any allowed delays)"

-- s 29(6): "Within 14 days after making a determination under subsection (1), the Commissioner must provide written
-- notice of the determination -- (a) to the complainant ...". Clock: day 0 is the day of the determination.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complaint party` `Complaint act`
`s 29(6) -- written notice of the determination within 14 days` MEANS
    PARTY  `the Commissioner`
    MUST   `give the complainant written notice of the determination`
    WITHIN 14
    LEST   BREACH BY `the Commissioner` BECAUSE "s 29(6): written notice of the determination was not given to the complainant within 14 days"

-- s 42(2): "an agreement is not binding unless it is -- (a) in writing; and (b) entered into within 14 days after the
-- verbal agreement is reached in the course of the conciliation." Clock: day 0 is the day of the verbal agreement.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complaint party` `Complaint act`
`s 42(2) -- the agreement must be entered into in writing within 14 days to be binding` MEANS
    PARTY  `the complainant and the provider`
    MUST   `enter into the conciliation agreement in writing`
    WITHIN 14
    LEST   BREACH BY `the complainant and the provider` BECAUSE "s 42(2): the agreement was not entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement, so it is not binding"

DECLARE `Deadline answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: done on or before the last day, which was` HAS `the last day allowed` IS A DATE
    `late: done after the last day, which was` HAS `the last day allowed` IS A DATE
    `not yet done, and still in time: the last day is` HAS `the last day allowed` IS A DATE
    `not yet done, and the last day has passed: it was` HAS `the last day allowed` IS A DATE

-- On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH: where the act was done, FULFILLED
-- means in time and anything else late; where it was not, the helper asks whether doing it today would still be in
-- time. It takes a DEONTIC value, so it is never exported.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Complaint party` `Complaint act`
      `the party` IS A `Complaint party`
      `the act` IS A `Complaint act`
      `the clock start` IS A DATE
      `the final day` IS A DATE
      `it has been done` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it was done` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Deadline answer`
`the deadline answer for` `the rule` `by` `the party` `doing` `the act` `from` `the clock start` `ending` `the final day` `done` `it has been done` `on` `the day it was done` `as of` `today` MEANS
    IF `it has been done`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the party` `the act` (`the days from` `the clock start` `to` `the day it was done`)))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `in time: done on or before the last day, which was` `the final day`
         OTHERWISE `late: done after the last day, which was` `the final day`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the party` `the act` (`the days from` `the clock start` `to` `today`)))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `not yet done, and still in time: the last day is` `the final day`
         OTHERWISE `not yet done, and the last day has passed: it was` `the final day`

§§ `s 27 -- the time for making a complaint`

-- s 27(3): "a complainant will be presumed to have notice of the circumstances giving rise to the complaint at the time
-- he or she might reasonably be expected to have had such notice." Fork F4: the day of notice is the earlier of the
-- day the complainant actually first had notice and the day they might reasonably be expected to have had it.
GIVEN `the day of actual notice` IS A DATE, `the day notice was reasonably to be expected` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 27 notice day from` `the day of actual notice` `and` `the day notice was reasonably to be expected` MEANS
    `the earlier of` `the day of actual notice` `and` `the day notice was reasonably to be expected`

GIVEN `the notice day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 27 last day after notice on` `the notice day` MEANS `the notice day` `plus months` 24

-- s 27(2): "The Commissioner may extend the period ... in a particular case -- (a) if satisfied that it is appropriate
-- to do so after taking into account -- (i) whether a proper investigation of the complaint should still be possible;
-- and (ii) whether the complaint should still be amenable to resolution ...; and (iii) whether it would be in the
-- public interest to entertain the complaint; and (iv) any other matter considered relevant by the Commissioner; or
-- (b) if -- (i) the complaint relates to the provision of a health or community service to a child; and (ii) the
-- complainant first had notice of the circumstances giving rise to the complaint after May 2004."
-- Fork F5: "after May 2004" is read as on or after 1 June 2004.
DECLARE `Complaint time answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: the complaint was made within 2 years from the day you first had notice (s 27(1)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the complaint` IS A DATE
    `still in time: make the complaint within 2 years from the day you first had notice (s 27(1)); the last day is` HAS `the last day for the complaint` IS A DATE
    `in time as extended: the Commissioner has extended the period in your case (s 27(2))`
    `out of time, but the Commissioner may extend the period, as the complaint is about a service to a child and you first had notice after May 2004 (s 27(2)(b)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the complaint` IS A DATE
    `out of time: the Commissioner may extend the period only if satisfied that it is appropriate, taking into account whether a proper investigation is still possible, whether the complaint is still amenable to resolution, the public interest and any other relevant matter (s 27(2)(a)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the complaint` IS A DATE

GIVEN `the day of actual notice` IS A DATE,
      `the day notice was reasonably to be expected` IS A DATE,
      `the complaint has been made` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the day the complaint was made` IS A DATE,
      `today` IS A DATE,
      `it is about a service to a child` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner has extended the period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Complaint time answer`
`the s 27 answer for notice on` `the day of actual notice` `or reasonably` `the day notice was reasonably to be expected` `made` `the complaint has been made` `on` `the day the complaint was made` `as of` `today` `child` `it is about a service to a child` `extended` `the Commissioner has extended the period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the deadline answer for` (`s 27(1) -- the complaint must be made within 2 years` (`the days from` `the s 27 day` `to` `the s 27 end`)) `by` `the complainant` `doing` `make the complaint` `from` `the s 27 day` `ending` `the s 27 end` `done` `the complaint has been made` `on` `the day the complaint was made` `as of` `today`
    WHEN `in time: done on or before the last day, which was` d THEN `in time: the complaint was made within 2 years from the day you first had notice (s 27(1)); the last day was` d
    WHEN `not yet done, and still in time: the last day is` d THEN `still in time: make the complaint within 2 years from the day you first had notice (s 27(1)); the last day is` d
    OTHERWISE
        BRANCH
            IF `the Commissioner has extended the period`
            THEN `in time as extended: the Commissioner has extended the period in your case (s 27(2))`
            IF `it is about a service to a child` AND ((`the date` 1 6 2004) `is on or before` `the s 27 day`)
            THEN `out of time, but the Commissioner may extend the period, as the complaint is about a service to a child and you first had notice after May 2004 (s 27(2)(b)); the last day was` `the s 27 end`
            OTHERWISE `out of time: the Commissioner may extend the period only if satisfied that it is appropriate, taking into account whether a proper investigation is still possible, whether the complaint is still amenable to resolution, the public interest and any other relevant matter (s 27(2)(a)); the last day was` `the s 27 end`
    WHERE
        `the s 27 day` MEANS `the s 27 notice day from` `the day of actual notice` `and` `the day notice was reasonably to be expected`
        `the s 27 end` MEANS `the s 27 last day after notice on` `the s 27 day`

§§ `s 28 with s 33(1)(h) -- the Commissioner's requirement for information`

DECLARE `Requirement answer` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you met the requirement within the period specified or extended (s 28(2), (3)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the requirement` IS A DATE
    `still in time: meet the requirement by the last day of the period specified or extended (s 28(2), (3)), which is` HAS `the last day for the requirement` IS A DATE
    `late, but with good reason: the Commissioner may act under s 33(1)(h) only on a failure without good reason; the Commissioner may still extend the period after it has expired (s 28(3)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the requirement` IS A DATE
    `late: if you failed without good reason, the Commissioner may determine to take no further action on, or to suspend action on, your complaint (s 33(1)(h)), and may still extend the period after it has expired (s 28(3)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the requirement` IS A DATE

-- Fork F6: s 33(1)(h) speaks of failing to comply "within a reasonable time"; the period the Commissioner must specify
-- as "reasonable" (s 28(2)) is taken to be that time.
GIVEN `the day of the requirement` IS A DATE,
      `the last day specified` IS A DATE,
      `it has been met` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the day it was met` IS A DATE,
      `today` IS A DATE,
      `there was a good reason` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Requirement answer`
`the s 28 answer for a requirement on` `the day of the requirement` `ending` `the last day specified` `met` `it has been met` `on` `the day it was met` `as of` `today` `good reason` `there was a good reason` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the deadline answer for` (`s 28 -- the requirement must be met within the period specified` (`the days from` `the day of the requirement` `to` `the last day specified`)) `by` `the complainant` `doing` `meet the Commissioner's requirement for information, documents or a statutory declaration` `from` `the day of the requirement` `ending` `the last day specified` `done` `it has been met` `on` `the day it was met` `as of` `today`
    WHEN `in time: done on or before the last day, which was` d THEN `in time: you met the requirement within the period specified or extended (s 28(2), (3)); the last day was` d
    WHEN `not yet done, and still in time: the last day is` d THEN `still in time: meet the requirement by the last day of the period specified or extended (s 28(2), (3)), which is` d
    OTHERWISE
        IF `there was a good reason`
        THEN `late, but with good reason: the Commissioner may act under s 33(1)(h) only on a failure without good reason; the Commissioner may still extend the period after it has expired (s 28(3)); the last day was` `the last day specified`
        ELSE `late: if you failed without good reason, the Commissioner may determine to take no further action on, or to suspend action on, your complaint (s 33(1)(h)), and may still extend the period after it has expired (s 28(3)); the last day was` `the last day specified`

§§ `s 29 -- assessment, and notice of the determination`

GIVEN `the day received` IS A DATE, `the days of allowed delay` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 29(1) last day for a complaint received on` `the day received` `with delay` `the days of allowed delay` MEANS
    `the day received` `plus days` (45 PLUS `the days of allowed delay`)

GIVEN `the day received` IS A DATE,
      `the days of allowed delay` IS A NUMBER,
      `it has been determined` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the day of the determination` IS A DATE,
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Deadline answer`
`the s 29(1) answer for a complaint received on` `the day received` `with delay` `the days of allowed delay` `determined` `it has been determined` `on` `the day of the determination` `as of` `today` MEANS
    `the deadline answer for` (`s 29(1) -- the Commissioner must assess the complaint within 45 days` (45 PLUS `the days of allowed delay`)) `by` `the Commissioner` `doing` `assess the complaint and make a determination` `from` `the day received` `ending` (`the s 29(1) last day for a complaint received on` `the day received` `with delay` `the days of allowed delay`) `done` `it has been determined` `on` `the day of the determination` `as of` `today`

GIVEN `the day of the determination` IS A DATE,
      `notice has been given` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the day notice was given` IS A DATE,
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Deadline answer`
`the s 29(6) answer for a determination on` `the day of the determination` `notified` `notice has been given` `on` `the day notice was given` `as of` `today` MEANS
    `the deadline answer for` `s 29(6) -- written notice of the determination within 14 days` `by` `the Commissioner` `doing` `give the complainant written notice of the determination` `from` `the day of the determination` `ending` (`the day of the determination` `plus days` 14) `done` `notice has been given` `on` `the day notice was given` `as of` `today`

-- s 29(5): "A complaint should not proceed under this Act if it appears to the Commissioner that the complainant has
-- failed, without good reason, to take reasonable steps to resolve the matter with the relevant health or community
-- service provider before making the complaint."
DECLARE `First steps answer` IS ONE OF
    `it may proceed: you took reasonable steps to resolve the matter with the provider first (s 29(5))`
    `it may proceed: you had good reason for not first trying to resolve the matter with the provider (s 29(5))`
    `it should not proceed, if it appears to the Commissioner that you failed, without good reason, to take reasonable steps to resolve the matter with the provider before complaining (s 29(5))`

GIVEN `I took reasonable steps with the provider first` IS A BOOLEAN, `I had good reason not to` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `First steps answer`
`the s 29(5) answer for` `I took reasonable steps with the provider first` `good reason` `I had good reason not to` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `I took reasonable steps with the provider first`
        THEN `it may proceed: you took reasonable steps to resolve the matter with the provider first (s 29(5))`
        IF `I had good reason not to`
        THEN `it may proceed: you had good reason for not first trying to resolve the matter with the provider (s 29(5))`
        OTHERWISE `it should not proceed, if it appears to the Commissioner that you failed, without good reason, to take reasonable steps to resolve the matter with the provider before complaining (s 29(5))`

§§ `ss 29(4), 30(7), (8), (11), 39(3), (4) -- conciliation and informal mediation`

DECLARE `Conciliation answer` IS ONE OF
    `it may: the Commissioner may refer the complaint to a conciliator (s 29(2)(a)) or assist the parties through informal mediation (s 30(7)); neither is required, and either must end at your request (ss 30(11)(b), 39(4))`
    `no: the Commissioner must not refer the complaint to a conciliator, and informal mediation should not occur, because it appears to indicate a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance or a significant question as to a provider's practice (ss 29(4), 30(8)); a conciliation or mediation already begun must be brought to an end (ss 30(11)(a), 39(3))`

GIVEN `it appears to raise a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `it appears to raise a significant question as to a provider's practice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Conciliation answer`
`the conciliation answer for` `it appears to raise a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance` `practice` `it appears to raise a significant question as to a provider's practice` MEANS
    IF `it appears to raise a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance` OR `it appears to raise a significant question as to a provider's practice`
    THEN `no: the Commissioner must not refer the complaint to a conciliator, and informal mediation should not occur, because it appears to indicate a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance or a significant question as to a provider's practice (ss 29(4), 30(8)); a conciliation or mediation already begun must be brought to an end (ss 30(11)(a), 39(3))`
    ELSE `it may: the Commissioner may refer the complaint to a conciliator (s 29(2)(a)) or assist the parties through informal mediation (s 30(7)); neither is required, and either must end at your request (ss 30(11)(b), 39(4))`

§§ `s 37 -- help and representation at conciliation`

-- s 37: "(1) A party to a conciliation may be assisted by another person. (2) However, a party cannot be assisted by a
-- particular person if the conciliator has directed that that person not be allowed to act in the particular matter.
-- (3) A party in a conciliation process may not be represented by another person except where the Commissioner is
-- satisfied that such representation is likely to assist substantially in resolving the complaint."
-- Fork F7: the s 37(2) direction is read as reaching assistance only, as its words say, not representation.
DECLARE `Kind of help` IS ONE OF `assisting me`, `representing me`

DECLARE `Help answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a party to a conciliation may be assisted by another person (s 37(1))`
    `no: the conciliator has directed that this person not be allowed to act in the matter (s 37(2))`
    `yes: you may be represented, as the Commissioner is satisfied that representation is likely to assist substantially in resolving the complaint (s 37(3))`
    `no: a party may not be represented except where the Commissioner is satisfied that representation is likely to assist substantially in resolving the complaint (s 37(3))`

GIVEN `the help` IS A `Kind of help`,
      `the conciliator has directed that this person not act` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner is satisfied representation is likely to assist substantially` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Help answer`
`the s 37 answer for` `the help` `direction` `the conciliator has directed that this person not act` `satisfied` `the Commissioner is satisfied representation is likely to assist substantially` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the help`
    WHEN `assisting me` THEN
        IF `the conciliator has directed that this person not act`
        THEN `no: the conciliator has directed that this person not be allowed to act in the matter (s 37(2))`
        ELSE `yes: a party to a conciliation may be assisted by another person (s 37(1))`
    WHEN `representing me` THEN
        IF `the Commissioner is satisfied representation is likely to assist substantially`
        THEN `yes: you may be represented, as the Commissioner is satisfied that representation is likely to assist substantially in resolving the complaint (s 37(3))`
        ELSE `no: a party may not be represented except where the Commissioner is satisfied that representation is likely to assist substantially in resolving the complaint (s 37(3))`

§§ `ss 30(10), 40(1), 45(6) -- what is said in mediation or conciliation, in other proceedings`

-- s 30(10): "Anything said or done during an informal mediation, other than something that reveals a significant issue
-- of public safety, interest or importance, is not to be disclosed in any other proceedings (whether under this or any
-- other Act or law) except by consent of the parties." s 40(1): "Anything said or done during conciliation under this
-- Part, or information provided for the purposes of conciliation under this Part, other than ..., is not to be
-- disclosed in any other proceedings ... except by consent of all parties to the conciliation." s 45(6): the same for
-- conciliation attempted during an investigation, "except by consent of all parties to the conciliation".
DECLARE `Kind of process` IS ONE OF
    `informal mediation during a preliminary inquiry`
    `conciliation by a conciliator`
    `conciliation attempted during an investigation`

DECLARE `Disclosure answer` IS ONE OF
    `the bar does not cover it: it reveals a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance` HAS `the disclosure provision` IS A STRING
    `yes, by consent: the parties have all consented to its disclosure` HAS `the disclosure provision` IS A STRING
    `no: anything said or done in it is not to be disclosed in any other proceedings, under any Act or law, without the parties' consent` HAS `the disclosure provision` IS A STRING

GIVEN `the process` IS A `Kind of process`,
      `it reveals a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `all the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Disclosure answer`
`the disclosure answer for` `the process` `significant` `it reveals a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance` `consent` `all the parties consent` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `it reveals a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance`
        THEN `the bar does not cover it: it reveals a significant issue of public safety, interest or importance` `the provision`
        IF `all the parties consent`
        THEN `yes, by consent: the parties have all consented to its disclosure` `the provision`
        OTHERWISE `no: anything said or done in it is not to be disclosed in any other proceedings, under any Act or law, without the parties' consent` `the provision`
    WHERE
        `the provision` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the process`
            WHEN `informal mediation during a preliminary inquiry` THEN "s 30(10)"
            WHEN `conciliation by a conciliator` THEN "s 40(1)"
            OTHERWISE "s 45(6)"

§§ `s 42 -- is the conciliation agreement binding`

DECLARE `Agreement answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 42 does not affect it: an agreement reached outside the conciliation process is as effective as it otherwise would be (s 42(3))`
    `it can be binding: it was entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement (s 42(1), (2)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the agreement` IS A DATE
    `not binding: it was not entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement (s 42(2)); the last day was` HAS `the last day for the agreement` IS A DATE
    `not binding yet: to be binding it must be entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement (s 42(2)); the last day is` HAS `the last day for the agreement` IS A DATE

GIVEN `it was reached in conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the day of the verbal agreement` IS A DATE,
      `it has been put in writing` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the day it was entered into in writing` IS A DATE,
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Agreement answer`
`the s 42 answer for` `it was reached in conciliation` `verbal on` `the day of the verbal agreement` `written` `it has been put in writing` `on` `the day it was entered into in writing` `as of` `today` MEANS
    IF NOT (`it was reached in conciliation`)
    THEN `s 42 does not affect it: an agreement reached outside the conciliation process is as effective as it otherwise would be (s 42(3))`
    ELSE
        CONSIDER `the deadline answer for` `s 42(2) -- the agreement must be entered into in writing within 14 days to be binding` `by` `the complainant and the provider` `doing` `enter into the conciliation agreement in writing` `from` `the day of the verbal agreement` `ending` (`the day of the verbal agreement` `plus days` 14) `done` `it has been put in writing` `on` `the day it was entered into in writing` `as of` `today`
        WHEN `in time: done on or before the last day, which was` d THEN `it can be binding: it was entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement (s 42(1), (2)); the last day was` d
        WHEN `not yet done, and still in time: the last day is` d THEN `not binding yet: to be binding it must be entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement (s 42(2)); the last day is` d
        WHEN `late: done after the last day, which was` d THEN `not binding: it was not entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement (s 42(2)); the last day was` d
        WHEN `not yet done, and the last day has passed: it was` d THEN `not binding: it was not entered into in writing within 14 days after the verbal agreement (s 42(2)); the last day was` d

§§ `s 33 -- when the Commissioner stops or suspends action`

-- s 33(2): "must take no further action ... if ... satisfied that all issues arising out of the subject matter of the
-- complaint have been adjudicated by a court, tribunal, authority or other person or body ..."; s 33(3): "must suspend
-- action ... if ... satisfied -- (a) that proceedings that relate to the subject matter of the complaint have been
-- commenced before the court; or (b) that an inquest ... has been commenced by a coroner"; s 33(1): "may ... determine
-- to take no further action ..., or to suspend action ..., if the Commissioner considers or is satisfied that -- (a)
-- ... (k) ..."; s 33(4): written notice to the complainant; s 33(5): recommencing a suspended complaint.
-- Fork F8: "before the court" in s 33(3)(a) is read as before a court; proceedings before a tribunal, authority or
-- other body fall under s 33(1)(d).
DECLARE `Stopping ground` IS ONE OF
    `none of the s 33(1) grounds`
    `I am not entitled to make the complaint`
    `the complaint does not disclose a ground of complaint`
    `the matter should be determined by legal proceedings`
    `I have been given reasonable explanations or information and there is no benefit in going on`
    `I relied on a ground I should have disclosed earlier`
    `the complaint lacks substance, is unnecessary, unjustifiable, frivolous, vexatious or not in good faith`
    `I failed without good reason to comply with a requirement or take a reasonable step`
    `going on would be an abuse of the Act's processes`
    `the complaint has been resolved or abandoned`

DECLARE `Stopping answer` IS ONE OF
    `the Commissioner must take no further action: all issues arising out of the subject matter have been adjudicated (s 33(2)); you must be given written notice (s 33(4))`
    `the Commissioner must suspend action: court proceedings or a coroner's inquest on the subject matter have been commenced (s 33(3)); you must be given written notice (s 33(4)), and action may be recommenced for any reasonable cause (s 33(5))`
    `the Commissioner may determine to take no further action, or to suspend action, with written notice to you (s 33(1), (4)), on the ground in` HAS `the s 33 ground` IS A STRING
    `only for some other reasonable cause that justifies discontinuing or suspending (s 33(1)(k)): none of the other grounds in s 33 is present`

GIVEN `all issues have been adjudicated` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `court proceedings on the subject matter have been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `a coroner's inquest on the subject matter has been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proceedings before a tribunal, authority or other body have been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the ground the Commissioner considers present` IS A `Stopping ground`
GIVETH A `Stopping answer`
`the s 33 answer for` `all issues have been adjudicated` `court` `court proceedings on the subject matter have been commenced` `inquest` `a coroner's inquest on the subject matter has been commenced` `other proceedings` `proceedings before a tribunal, authority or other body have been commenced` `ground` `the ground the Commissioner considers present` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `all issues have been adjudicated`
        THEN `the Commissioner must take no further action: all issues arising out of the subject matter have been adjudicated (s 33(2)); you must be given written notice (s 33(4))`
        IF `court proceedings on the subject matter have been commenced` OR `a coroner's inquest on the subject matter has been commenced`
        THEN `the Commissioner must suspend action: court proceedings or a coroner's inquest on the subject matter have been commenced (s 33(3)); you must be given written notice (s 33(4)), and action may be recommenced for any reasonable cause (s 33(5))`
        IF `proceedings before a tribunal, authority or other body have been commenced`
        THEN `the Commissioner may determine to take no further action, or to suspend action, with written notice to you (s 33(1), (4)), on the ground in` "s 33(1)(d)"
        IF `the s 33(1) paragraph` EQUALS ""
        THEN `only for some other reasonable cause that justifies discontinuing or suspending (s 33(1)(k)): none of the other grounds in s 33 is present`
        OTHERWISE `the Commissioner may determine to take no further action, or to suspend action, with written notice to you (s 33(1), (4)), on the ground in` `the s 33(1) paragraph`
    WHERE
        `the s 33(1) paragraph` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the ground the Commissioner considers present`
            WHEN `I am not entitled to make the complaint` THEN "s 33(1)(a)"
            WHEN `the complaint does not disclose a ground of complaint` THEN "s 33(1)(b)"
            WHEN `the matter should be determined by legal proceedings` THEN "s 33(1)(c)"
            WHEN `I have been given reasonable explanations or information and there is no benefit in going on` THEN "s 33(1)(e)"
            WHEN `I relied on a ground I should have disclosed earlier` THEN "s 33(1)(f), with s 26(2)"
            WHEN `the complaint lacks substance, is unnecessary, unjustifiable, frivolous, vexatious or not in good faith` THEN "s 33(1)(g)"
            WHEN `I failed without good reason to comply with a requirement or take a reasonable step` THEN "s 33(1)(h)"
            WHEN `going on would be an abuse of the Act's processes` THEN "s 33(1)(i)"
            WHEN `the complaint has been resolved or abandoned` THEN "s 33(1)(j)"
            OTHERWISE ""

§§ `s 34 -- withdrawing a complaint`

-- s 34: "(1) A complainant may withdraw a complaint at any time by written notice to the Commissioner. (2) If a
-- complaint is withdrawn -- (a) any investigation ... will cease unless the Commissioner has determined to conduct or
-- continue an investigation under section 43(1)(c) ... (3) If the Commissioner has referred a complaint to another
-- person or body ..., the withdrawal ... does not affect the performance and exercise by the person or body of his, her
-- or its functions and powers in respect of the matters raised ..."
DECLARE `Withdrawal answer` IS ONE OF
    `not withdrawn: a complaint is withdrawn by written notice to the Commissioner, which you may give at any time (s 34(1))`
    `withdrawn: any investigation of it ceases (s 34(2)(a))`
    `withdrawn, but the investigation of the significant issue or question it raised may go on (ss 34(2)(a), 43(1)(c), (2)(c))`
    `withdrawn: any investigation of it ceases, but the person or body it was referred to may still act on the matters it raised (s 34(2)(a), (3))`
    `withdrawn, but the investigation of the significant issue or question may go on, and the person or body it was referred to may still act on the matters it raised (ss 34(2)(a), (3), 43(1)(c))`

GIVEN `I gave written notice of withdrawal` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner has determined to investigate under s 43(1)(c)` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the complaint was referred to another person or body` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Withdrawal answer`
`the s 34 answer for` `I gave written notice of withdrawal` `s 43(1)(c)` `the Commissioner has determined to investigate under s 43(1)(c)` `referred` `the complaint was referred to another person or body` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`I gave written notice of withdrawal`)
        THEN `not withdrawn: a complaint is withdrawn by written notice to the Commissioner, which you may give at any time (s 34(1))`
        IF `the Commissioner has determined to investigate under s 43(1)(c)` AND `the complaint was referred to another person or body`
        THEN `withdrawn, but the investigation of the significant issue or question may go on, and the person or body it was referred to may still act on the matters it raised (ss 34(2)(a), (3), 43(1)(c))`
        IF `the Commissioner has determined to investigate under s 43(1)(c)`
        THEN `withdrawn, but the investigation of the significant issue or question it raised may go on (ss 34(2)(a), 43(1)(c), (2)(c))`
        IF `the complaint was referred to another person or body`
        THEN `withdrawn: any investigation of it ceases, but the person or body it was referred to may still act on the matters it raised (s 34(2)(a), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `withdrawn: any investigation of it ceases (s 34(2)(a))`

§§ `ss 28A, 29(3), 57(5), 60(8) -- who deals with the complaint`

-- s 28A(1): "the Commissioner must refer a complaint that is a prescribed child protection complaint to the
-- Ombudsman"; (2): unless it is in a class an administrative arrangement leaves with the Commissioner; (8): a
-- prescribed child protection complaint "means -- (a) a complaint relating to a health or community service -- (i)
-- that is provided to, or for the benefit of, a child who may be, or who has been, at risk; or (ii) that consists of,
-- or includes, a notification ... of a suspicion that a child may be at risk; or (iii) that consists of, or includes or
-- arises out of, an investigation of a case where a child may be at risk; or (iv) that is provided to, or for the
-- benefit of, a child who is under the guardianship, or in the custody of, the Minister or another person under the
-- Children and Young People (Safety) Act 2016, where the provision of the service constitutes an administrative act on
-- the part of an agency to which the Ombudsman Act 1972 applies; or (b) any other complaint of a kind declared by the
-- regulations ..." No kind is declared by the regulations deposited.
DECLARE `Child protection link` IS ONE OF
    `a service to or for a child who may be or has been at risk`
    `a notification of a suspicion that a child may be at risk`
    `an investigation of a case where a child may be at risk`
    `a service to or for a child under the guardianship or in the custody of the Minister or another person under the Children and Young People (Safety) Act 2016`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Ombudsman answer` IS ONE OF
    `it goes to the Ombudsman: it is a prescribed child protection complaint, which the Commissioner must refer to the Ombudsman to be dealt with under the Ombudsman Act 1972 (s 28A(1), (8))`
    `the Commissioner deals with it: it is a prescribed child protection complaint, but in a class the administrative arrangement with the Ombudsman leaves with the Commissioner (s 28A(2))`
    `no: it is not a prescribed child protection complaint (s 28A(8), a "means" definition), so s 28A does not send it to the Ombudsman`

GIVEN `the link` IS A `Child protection link`,
      `the service is an administrative act of an agency under the Ombudsman Act 1972` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `it is in a class the arrangement leaves with the Commissioner` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Ombudsman answer`
`the s 28A answer for` `the link` `administrative act` `the service is an administrative act of an agency under the Ombudsman Act 1972` `arrangement` `it is in a class the arrangement leaves with the Commissioner` MEANS
    IF (NOT (`the link` EQUALS `none of these`)) AND `the service is an administrative act of an agency under the Ombudsman Act 1972`
    THEN IF `it is in a class the arrangement leaves with the Commissioner`
         THEN `the Commissioner deals with it: it is a prescribed child protection complaint, but in a class the administrative arrangement with the Ombudsman leaves with the Commissioner (s 28A(2))`
         ELSE `it goes to the Ombudsman: it is a prescribed child protection complaint, which the Commissioner must refer to the Ombudsman to be dealt with under the Ombudsman Act 1972 (s 28A(1), (8))`
    ELSE `no: it is not a prescribed child protection complaint (s 28A(8), a "means" definition), so s 28A does not send it to the Ombudsman`

-- s 57(5): "If a complaint relates to a nationally registered health practitioner, the matter will proceed under Part
-- 8 of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (South Australia) rather than under a preceding subsection.";
-- s 57(1)-(4): for another registered service provider, the Commissioner "should consult with the relevant
-- registration authority" and the complaint may be referred to it; s 29(3): for an approved provider under the Aged
-- Care Act 1997 (Cth), the Commissioner "must consult with the relevant complaints resolution bodies under that Act" and
-- may refer the complaint. ("Approved provider" is the Commonwealth term; it is asked as a fact.)
DECLARE `Kind of provider` IS ONE OF
    `a nationally registered health practitioner`
    `another registered service provider`
    `an approved provider under the Aged Care Act 1997 of the Commonwealth`
    `a provider that is not registered`

DECLARE `Handling answer` IS ONE OF
    `the matter proceeds under Part 8 of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (South Australia), not under s 57(1) to (4) (s 57(5))`
    `the Commissioner should consult the registration authority about managing it, and it may be referred to that authority if both agree (s 57(1) to (4)); the authority may also take interim measures on the provider's right to practise (s 65)`
    `the Commissioner must consult the Commonwealth aged care complaints resolution bodies, and may refer it to another authority under that Act (s 29(3))`
    `the Commissioner assesses it and may conciliate, investigate, refer or take no further action (s 29(2))`

GIVEN `the provider` IS A `Kind of provider`
GIVETH A `Handling answer`
`the handling answer for` `the provider` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the provider`
    WHEN `a nationally registered health practitioner` THEN `the matter proceeds under Part 8 of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (South Australia), not under s 57(1) to (4) (s 57(5))`
    WHEN `another registered service provider` THEN `the Commissioner should consult the registration authority about managing it, and it may be referred to that authority if both agree (s 57(1) to (4)); the authority may also take interim measures on the provider's right to practise (s 65)`
    WHEN `an approved provider under the Aged Care Act 1997 of the Commonwealth` THEN `the Commissioner must consult the Commonwealth aged care complaints resolution bodies, and may refer it to another authority under that Act (s 29(3))`
    OTHERWISE `the Commissioner assesses it and may conciliate, investigate, refer or take no further action (s 29(2))`

§§ `ss 54, 55, 56D -- what the complainant is given after an investigation`

DECLARE `Kind of outcome document` IS ONE OF
    `a report of the investigation's findings and conclusions`
    `a notice of recommended action to the provider`
    `a report the Commissioner publishes after a notice of recommended action`
    `a statement of action taken against the provider under s 56C`

DECLARE `Copy answer` IS ONE OF
    `not as of right: the Commissioner may give copies of an investigation report to such persons as the Commissioner thinks fit (s 54(2))`
    `you are to be advised that the notice was given to the provider, if the Commissioner gives one (s 55(1)(b)); the provider has at least 28 days to respond (s 55(5))`
    `yes: the Commissioner must give a copy of the report to any complainant who was a party to the proceedings (s 55(10))`
    `yes, if the action relates to your complaint: the Commissioner must give you a copy of the statement (s 56D(2)(a))`
    `no: the Commissioner must give a copy of a s 56D statement to the complainant only where the action relates to a complaint (s 56D(2)(a))`

GIVEN `the document` IS A `Kind of outcome document`, `it relates to my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Copy answer`
`the copy answer for` `the document` `relates` `it relates to my complaint` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the document`
    WHEN `a report of the investigation's findings and conclusions` THEN `not as of right: the Commissioner may give copies of an investigation report to such persons as the Commissioner thinks fit (s 54(2))`
    WHEN `a notice of recommended action to the provider` THEN `you are to be advised that the notice was given to the provider, if the Commissioner gives one (s 55(1)(b)); the provider has at least 28 days to respond (s 55(5))`
    WHEN `a report the Commissioner publishes after a notice of recommended action` THEN `yes: the Commissioner must give a copy of the report to any complainant who was a party to the proceedings (s 55(10))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `it relates to my complaint`
        THEN `yes, if the action relates to your complaint: the Commissioner must give you a copy of the statement (s 56D(2)(a))`
        ELSE `no: the Commissioner must give a copy of a s 56D statement to the complainant only where the action relates to a complaint (s 56D(2)(a))`

§§ `ss 56B, 56C, 56F with reg 8 -- orders against a health service provider`

-- s 56B(1): the Commissioner may act if "(a) an investigation has been commenced ... in relation to a person who has
-- provided a health service; and (b) the Commissioner has a reasonable belief that the person has -- (i) breached a
-- prescribed code of conduct under section 56A that applies to the person; or (ii) committed a prescribed offence; and
-- (c) in the opinion of the Commissioner action under this section is necessary to protect the health or safety of
-- members of the public"; (2)(a): a prohibition order "for a period of 12 weeks or such shorter period as may be
-- specified"; (5): contravening an order: "Maximum penalty: $10 000 or imprisonment for 2 years or both."
-- s 56C(1): "(a) an investigation has been carried out ...; and (b) the Commissioner is satisfied that the person has
-- -- (i) breached a prescribed code of conduct ...; or (ii) been found guilty of a prescribed offence; and (c) in the
-- opinion of the Commissioner the person poses an unacceptable risk to the health or safety of members of the public
-- ..."; (2)(a): an order "for a specified period or indefinitely"; (4): the same maximum penalty.
-- s 56F(2): "action may not be taken under this Division in relation to conduct that falls within the ambit of Part 7".
-- reg 8: the prescribed offences, for both sections: an offence against the Australian Consumer Law (SA); Part 3 of the
-- Criminal Law Consolidation Act 1935; Part 7 Division 10 of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (South
-- Australia); the South Australian Public Health Act 2011. reg 7 and Sch 2: the code of conduct for health care workers.
DECLARE `Provider conduct` IS ONE OF
    `a breach of the prescribed code of conduct that applies to them`
    `an offence against the Australian Consumer Law (SA)`
    `an offence against Part 3 of the Criminal Law Consolidation Act 1935`
    `an offence against Part 7 Division 10 of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (South Australia)`
    `an offence against the South Australian Public Health Act 2011`
    `another offence`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Investigation stage` IS ONE OF
    `no investigation`
    `an investigation has been commenced`
    `an investigation has been carried out`

DECLARE `Order answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: action under Division 5 may not be taken on conduct within Part 7 (the registration authorities' part) (s 56F(2))`
    `yes: the Commissioner may make an order prohibiting the provider from providing health services (or imposing conditions) for a specified period or indefinitely, and publish a public statement (s 56C(2)); contravening the order carries a maximum penalty of $10 000 or 2 years' imprisonment or both (s 56C(4)); if it relates to your complaint you get a copy of the statement of reasons (s 56D(2)(a))`
    `yes: the Commissioner may make an interim order prohibiting the provider from providing health services (or imposing conditions) for 12 weeks or a shorter period, and publish a public statement (s 56B(2)); contravening the order carries a maximum penalty of $10 000 or 2 years' imprisonment or both (s 56B(5))`
    `ss 56B and 56C do not reach this: neither section's conditions are all met on these facts`

GIVEN `the conduct is within Part 7` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the stage` IS AN `Investigation stage`,
      `the conduct` IS A `Provider conduct`,
      `the Commissioner reasonably believes it happened` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner is satisfied of the breach, or the provider was found guilty` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner thinks action is necessary to protect the public` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Commissioner thinks the provider poses an unacceptable risk` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Order answer`
`the order answer for` `the conduct is within Part 7` `stage` `the stage` `conduct` `the conduct` `believes` `the Commissioner reasonably believes it happened` `satisfied or guilty` `the Commissioner is satisfied of the breach, or the provider was found guilty` `necessary` `the Commissioner thinks action is necessary to protect the public` `unacceptable risk` `the Commissioner thinks the provider poses an unacceptable risk` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the conduct is within Part 7`
        THEN `no: action under Division 5 may not be taken on conduct within Part 7 (the registration authorities' part) (s 56F(2))`
        IF (`the stage` EQUALS `an investigation has been carried out`) AND `the conduct is prescribed` AND `the Commissioner is satisfied of the breach, or the provider was found guilty` AND `the Commissioner thinks the provider poses an unacceptable risk`
        THEN `yes: the Commissioner may make an order prohibiting the provider from providing health services (or imposing conditions) for a specified period or indefinitely, and publish a public statement (s 56C(2)); contravening the order carries a maximum penalty of $10 000 or 2 years' imprisonment or both (s 56C(4)); if it relates to your complaint you get a copy of the statement of reasons (s 56D(2)(a))`
        IF (NOT (`the stage` EQUALS `no investigation`)) AND `the conduct is prescribed` AND `the Commissioner reasonably believes it happened` AND `the Commissioner thinks action is necessary to protect the public`
        THEN `yes: the Commissioner may make an interim order prohibiting the provider from providing health services (or imposing conditions) for 12 weeks or a shorter period, and publish a public statement (s 56B(2)); contravening the order carries a maximum penalty of $10 000 or 2 years' imprisonment or both (s 56B(5))`
        OTHERWISE `ss 56B and 56C do not reach this: neither section's conditions are all met on these facts`
    WHERE
        `the conduct is prescribed` MEANS (NOT (`the conduct` EQUALS `another offence`)) AND (NOT (`the conduct` EQUALS `none of these`))

§§ `s 74 -- keeping the complainant's identity from the provider`

-- s 74: the Commissioner "may determine not to disclose to a health or community service provider information that
-- would enable a health or community service consumer or a complainant to be identified if -- (a) the Commissioner
-- believes, on reasonable grounds, that the disclosure would, directly or indirectly -- (i) put at risk the health or
-- safety of a person; or (ii) result in a person receiving a health or community service of a lower standard ...; or
-- (iii) prejudice the fair assessment of a complaint or an investigation ...; or (b) in the Commissioner's opinion --
-- (i) the complaint -- (A) raises a significant issue of public safety, public interest or public importance; or (B)
-- raises a significant question as to the practice of a health or community service provider; and (ii) non-disclosure
-- is in the public interest."
DECLARE `Identity answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes, the Commissioner may withhold it: the Commissioner believes on reasonable grounds that disclosure would put a person's health or safety at risk, lower the standard of service a person receives, or prejudice the fair assessment of the complaint (s 74(a))`
    `yes, the Commissioner may withhold it: the complaint raises a significant public issue or question as to a provider's practice, and non-disclosure is in the public interest (s 74(b))`
    `s 74 does not reach this: the Commissioner may withhold your identity from the provider only in the circumstances it states`

GIVEN `the Commissioner believes disclosure would cause one of the s 74(a) harms` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the complaint raises a significant public issue or question as to practice` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `non-disclosure is in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH AN `Identity answer`
`the s 74 answer for` `the Commissioner believes disclosure would cause one of the s 74(a) harms` `significant` `the complaint raises a significant public issue or question as to practice` `public interest` `non-disclosure is in the public interest` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the Commissioner believes disclosure would cause one of the s 74(a) harms`
        THEN `yes, the Commissioner may withhold it: the Commissioner believes on reasonable grounds that disclosure would put a person's health or safety at risk, lower the standard of service a person receives, or prejudice the fair assessment of the complaint (s 74(a))`
        IF `the complaint raises a significant public issue or question as to practice` AND `non-disclosure is in the public interest`
        THEN `yes, the Commissioner may withhold it: the complaint raises a significant public issue or question as to a provider's practice, and non-disclosure is in the public interest (s 74(b))`
        OTHERWISE `s 74 does not reach this: the Commissioner may withhold your identity from the provider only in the circumstances it states`

§§ `ss 78, 79 -- intimidation and reprisals`

-- s 78: "A person must not persuade or attempt to persuade by threat or intimidation another person -- (a) to refrain
-- from making a complaint ...; or (b) to withdraw a complaint ...; or (c) to fail to co-operate with the Commissioner
-- ...; or (d) to fail to provide information or a document ...; or (e) to provide information or a document that is
-- false or misleading ... Maximum penalty: $10 000." s 79(1): "A person must not treat another person unfavourably on
-- the ground that a person -- (a) has made a complaint ...; or (b) has co-operated with the Commissioner ...; or (c)
-- has provided information or documents ..., or on the ground that he or she knows that a person intends to do any of
-- these things, or suspects that a person has done, or intends to do, any of these things. Maximum penalty: $10 000.
-- (2) It is sufficient for a contravention of subsection (1) if the ground specified in subsection (1) is a
-- significant factor in inducing the person to take the particular action against the other person. (3) Unfavourable
-- treatment on the ground that a person -- (a) has made a false allegation; or (b) has not acted in good faith, does
-- not constitute a contravention of subsection (1)."
-- Fork F9: where the complaint (or co-operation, or information) was not even a significant factor in the treatment,
-- the treatment is taken not to be "on the ground" of it (s 79(2) says when it is enough, not when it is needed).
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `threats or intimidation to stop me complaining, make me withdraw, not co-operate, withhold information, or give false information`
    `unfavourable treatment because I complained, co-operated or gave information, or was thought about to`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Reprisal answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is an offence to persuade or try to persuade you by threat or intimidation in this way; maximum penalty $10 000 (s 78)`
    `yes: it is an offence to treat you unfavourably on that ground, and it is enough that it was a significant factor; maximum penalty $10 000 (s 79(1), (2))`
    `no: unfavourable treatment on the ground that you made a false allegation, or did not act in good faith, does not contravene s 79(1) (s 79(3))`
    `no: s 79(1) is about treatment on the ground of the complaint, co-operation or information, and that was not even a significant factor (s 79(1), (2))`
    `ss 78 and 79 do not make this an offence`

GIVEN `what was done` IS A `What was done to me`,
      `it was a significant factor` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `I made a false allegation or did not act in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Reprisal answer`
`the reprisal answer for` `what was done` `significant factor` `it was a significant factor` `bad faith` `I made a false allegation or did not act in good faith` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what was done`
    WHEN `threats or intimidation to stop me complaining, make me withdraw, not co-operate, withhold information, or give false information` THEN `yes: it is an offence to persuade or try to persuade you by threat or intimidation in this way; maximum penalty $10 000 (s 78)`
    WHEN `unfavourable treatment because I complained, co-operated or gave information, or was thought about to` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF `I made a false allegation or did not act in good faith`
            THEN `no: unfavourable treatment on the ground that you made a false allegation, or did not act in good faith, does not contravene s 79(1) (s 79(3))`
            IF `it was a significant factor`
            THEN `yes: it is an offence to treat you unfavourably on that ground, and it is enough that it was a significant factor; maximum penalty $10 000 (s 79(1), (2))`
            OTHERWISE `no: s 79(1) is about treatment on the ground of the complaint, co-operation or information, and that was not even a significant factor (s 79(1), (2))`
    OTHERWISE `ss 78 and 79 do not make this an offence`

§§ `s 83 -- protection from civil actions`

-- s 83(1): "A person is not liable in respect of loss, damage or injury of any kind suffered by another person as a
-- result of any of the following done in good faith: (a) the making of a complaint; (b) the making of a statement, the
-- provision of any information ... or the provision of any document for the purposes of this Act to the Commissioner, a
-- conciliator or another person authorised to receive [it] ..."
DECLARE `Liability answer` IS ONE OF
    `no: you are not liable for loss, damage or injury of any kind that another person suffers as a result of your complaint, or of statements, information or documents you gave under the Act, done in good faith (s 83(1)(a), (b))`
    `s 83 gives no protection on these facts: it covers only what is done in good faith`

GIVEN `I acted in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Liability answer`
`the s 83 answer for` `I acted in good faith` MEANS
    IF `I acted in good faith`
    THEN `no: you are not liable for loss, damage or injury of any kind that another person suffers as a result of your complaint, or of statements, information or documents you gave under the Act, done in good faith (s 83(1)(a), (b))`
    ELSE `s 83 gives no protection on these facts: it covers only what is done in good faith`
